		{
			description: [
				"&lThe Mystical White Flower&r is the endgame of all flowers. To calculate it's chance of growing, a following formula is used:"
				""
				"&o1/(<standard deviation of differently colored flowers> + 1) * 100%&r"
				""
				"If you don't know what standard deviation means, it's basically how the average amount one type of flower growing in your garden differs from the amount of other types of flowers growing in your garden. If you still don't get it, google it."
				""
				"The formula does not include flowers that are not present in your garden at all, those that cannot be planted, or white flowers themselves."
				""
				"To plant this flower, use (requires a petal of EVERTY COLOR except white which will be consumed):"
				""
				"&7turtle.&6plant&7(consts.&6PLANTS&7.&6WHITE_FLOWER&7)&r"
			]
			disable_toast: true
			icon: "botania:white_mystical_flower"
			id: "36E42433406FA65D"
			shape: "gear"
			size: 2.0d
			title: "Mystical White Flower"
			x: -5.5d
			y: 1.75d
		}
		{
			description: [
				"&lAs you could've guessed&r, most of the scripts you are going to be making for this modpack are going to somehow involve the controls of the turtle."
				""
				"Let's first go over an important concept in programming - imports. As coders, we oftentimes get lazy and do not want to retype all of the things we've already implemented, that's where modules come into the picture."
				""
				"Most often, \"modules\" refers to scripts, which don't do anything by themselves, but contain a bunch of definitions for variables and functions (aka insturctions/methods) that are meant to be used elsewhere in other scripts."
				""
				"With this pack, 2 modules are bundled by default - &9\"turtle\"&r and &9\"consts\"&r. You can also create ones yourself by doing the same things you would to create a script, then their names would be the name of the file without the \".lua\" suffix (note: the importing script and the module must be in the same directory, this shouldn't matter if you are storing everything in one place)"
				""
				"To then later use the things that a module provides, you can use the &6require&7()&r function, specifying the module name by passing it in. That will then return a value that includes all of the variables and functions within a module, which you can write into a variable similar to this:"
				""
				"&7turtle = &6require&7(&b\"turtle\"&7)&r"
				"&7consts = &6require&7(&b\"consts\"&7)&r"
				"&7yourmodule = &6require&7(&b\"yourmodule\"&7)&r"
				"{@pagebreak}"
				"Now, to actually control the turtle you will need the first builtin module that has been described here - &7turtle&r. The use for the other one (&7consts&r) is covered in other parts of this guidebook."
				""
				"&7turtle&r contains declarations for methods directly tied to the actions of the bot. Some notable self-explanatory ones include:"
				""
				"&7turtle.&6forward&7()&r"
				"&7turtle.&6back&7()&r"
				"&7turtle.&6turnLeft&7()&r"
				"&7turtle.&6turnRight&7()&r"
				""
				"There are also ones for planting and gathering flowers:"
				""
				"&7turtle.&6gather&7()&r"
				"&7turtle.&6plant&7()&r"
				""
				"NOTE: last one requires a value, related to the flower you want to plant being passed in, see other parts of this guidebook for how that works."
				""
				"Lastly, a couple of functions included in the &7turtle&r module return values relating to information about the world around your turtle. For more info on those, look at the \"Gathering Data\" section."
			]
			disable_toast: true
			icon: {
				Count: 1
				id: "computercraft:turtle_normal"
				tag: {
					RightUpgrade: "minecraft:diamond_hoe"
					RightUpgradeNbt: {
						Tag: {
							Damage: 0
						}
					}
				}
			}
			id: "69D3872FC3078591"
			shape: "square"
			size: 4.0d
			title: "Turtle Controls"
			x: 2.5d
			y: -8.25d
		}
		{
			description: [
				"&lWhile making scripts&r, it can sometimes be useful to gather information about the world surrounding your turtle. For that, there are a couple different instructions you can use."
				""
				"To get the current coordinates of the turtle, with the left-most top-most corner being point (0;0), you can use the following method:"
				""
				"&7x, y = turtle.&6getCoords&7()&r"
				""
				"This will return 2 values, which in this example are written to variables &7x&r and &7y&r (note that technically, in Minecraft's coordinate system, those are actually coordinates x and z, not y; feel free to change the names of the variables if that bugs you)."
				""
				"Both of these variables are numbers you can then use for &9if&r statements or &9while&r loops, for example:"
				""
				"&9if&7 (x == 0) {&r"
				"    &7turtle.&6plant&7(consts.&6PLANTS&7.&6GREEN_FLOWER&7)&r"
				"&7}&r"
				""
				"This piece of code will only plant green flowers, when the turtle is on the left-most blocks of your garden."
				""
				"Reminder that the more the X-coordinate, the more the turtle is to the right. The more the Y-coordinate, the more the turtle is to the \"bottom\" of the garden."
				"{@pagebreak}"
				"Another useful piece of data is where the turtle is facing, as you cannot move cardinally and can only use &7turtle.&6turnLeft&7()&r and &7turtle.&6turnRight&7()&r. For that you can use:"
				""
				"&7facing = turtle.&6getFacing&7()&r"
				""
				"In this case, we get a string return value and set it to variable &7facing&r. It can be one of either - \"east\", \"west\", \"south\", or \"north\"."
				""
				"South being down, north being up, west being left, and east being right from the position of the camera."
				""
				"Combine this method with an &9if&r or &9while&r statement to, for example, make a program that returns the turtle back to (0;0) no matter where it is or where it's facing."
				"{@pagebreak}"
				"A few methods are related to the blocks located beneath the turtle. 3 of them in fact:"
				""
				"&7grown = turtle.&6getGrown&7()&r"
				""
				"This method returns a boolean (&btrue&r or &bfalse&r) value based on whether the plant below the turtle has yet grown or not (if there is no plant below the turtle, this will return &bfalse&r). Incredibly useful for making simple gathering scripts."
				""
				"&7plant = turtle.&6getPlant&7()&r"
				"&7ground = turtle.&6getGround&7()&r"
				""
				"These methods return the flower and ground respectively in their string forms. So, for example - &9\"botania:mystical_black_flower\"&r."
				""
				"The strings do contain modid: prefixes as part of how Minecraft stores them, all of the plants in this modpack come from the \"botania\" mod so they will all have a \"botania:\" prefix. All grounds have a \"minecraft:\" prefix."
				"{@pagebreak}"
				"Lastly, if you are too lazy to change the variables related to the size of you garden every time you update it, you can get its maximum coordinate (+1, since they start at 0) by using the following function:"
				""
				"&7range = turtle.&6getRange&7()&r"
				""
				"This will return a number, corresponding to the following numbers for each garden expansion tier:"
				""
				"1x1 - 4"
				"2x2 - 8"
				"3x3 - 12"
				"4x4 - 16"
				""
				"This is useful for passing into the &9for&r loop, to go around the entirety of your territory. Kind of like this:"
				""
				"&9for&7 x = 0, turtle.&6getRange&7()&9 do&r"
				"    &7...&r"
				"&9end&r"
			]
			disable_toast: true
			icon: {
				Count: 1
				id: "ftbquests:custom_icon"
				tag: {
					Icon: "ftblibrary:icons/info"
				}
			}
			id: "36AC8D30D13D5643"
			shape: "square"
			size: 4.0d
			title: "Gathering Data"
			x: 2.5d
			y: -4.0d
		}
		{
			description: [
				"&lMany parts of this&r guide refer to so called \"adjacent\" blocks, be it adjacent plants or adjacent grounds of certain type."
				""
				"To clear up the confusion once and for all, here's a little infographic (shown as a top-down view of a sections of your garden):"
				""
				"&c[]&2[]&c[]"
				"&2[]&9[]&2[]"
				"&c[]&2[]&c[]"
				""
				"Shown in &9blue&r is the hypothetical flower that has some mechanic related to its adjacent blocks. Shown in &2green&r ARE the adjacent blocks. Shown in &cred&r are NOT adjacent blocks."
				""
				"Only direct neighbours count, diagonals are technically 2 blocks away, and thus are not adjacent."
				""
				"\"Adjacent flowers\" means blocks that are at the same height (global Minecraft y-coordinate) as the flower that has the corresponding mechanic. \"Adjacent grounds\" means blocks that are 1 block lower than the actual flower."
			]
			disable_toast: true
			icon: "minecraft:recovery_compass"
			id: "767750DFCC21FA6E"
			shape: "square"
			size: 4.0d
			title: "Adjacent Blocks"
			x: 2.5d
			y: 0.25d
		}
		{
			description: [
				"&lPatience is key&r for a lot of things in life, and this modpack is no exception."
				""
				"You will often find yourself waiting for flowers to grow or random events to happen, and if you code your scripts in a way that just continously does the same tasks over and over with no break in-between, you may find yourself waiting for a single more loop to complete before achieving the task at hand."
				""
				"Instead of that, you can use the very useful &6sleep&7()&r instruction included by the developers of CC: Tweaked to simply not do anything for some amount of seconds that you pass into it. For example:"
				""
				"&6sleep&7(5)&r"
				""
				"Insert this into your script to wait for 5 seconds, thus allowing flowers to start fully growing again before doing another loop of gathering and re-planting of your garden."
				""
				"This instruction can especially be useful for growing gray flowers, which you will get into later in this pack."
			]
			disable_toast: true
			icon: "minecraft:clock"
			id: "69FCCC56DB40406A"
			shape: "square"
			size: 4.0d
			title: "Wasting Less Time"
			x: 2.5d
			y: 4.5d
		}
	]
	title: "Documentation"
}
